data.detailAuthoring.setComponent('main',html`<main class="page"><div class="eyebrow">Technical detail · Data</div><h1>Text → token sequences</h1><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Document sources</td><td>Record provenance, permitted uses, dates, languages, quality filters, and mixture weights. Data selection affects coverage and bias.</td></tr><tr><td>Filter and deduplicate</td><td>Remove malformed or unwanted content and exact/near duplicates. Quality filters can change which groups and domains are represented.</td></tr><tr><td>Prevent leakage</td><td>Separate related documents or sources before constructing windows. Check overlap with evaluation sets; random token-level splits can leak nearly identical content.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose a tokenizer</td><td>Learn or select a vocabulary, often subword pieces or bytes. Tokens are not necessarily words. Vocabulary size affects sequence length and compute.</td></tr><tr><td>Build sequences</td><td>Split, truncate, or pack token streams to a context limit. Apply padding, boundary, attention, and loss masks according to the recipe.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Shift the targets by one position</h2><div class="formula"><code>Input: [A, B, C] → Targets: [B, C, D]</code></div><p>One training pass scores many next-token positions in parallel, while causal masking limits each position to its permitted prefix.</p><p class="note">A document-boundary token alone does not prevent attention across packed documents. Keep the tokenizer and special-token conventions with the model weights: token IDs have model-specific meanings.</p></main>`,style);
